 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "POULNABRONE. A portal tomb of around 3800 BC, so older than the pyramids. The name means 'hole of the quern stones' in Irish. Remains of more than thirty people were found under it. A handsome roof, in its way."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "CLIFFS OF MOHER. Fourteen kilometres of sea cliff, up to 214 metres high at the tallest. Nobody has ever been disappointed. A fair few have been blown sideways."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "O'BRIEN'S TOWER. Built in 1835 by Cornelius O'Brien, so that visitors would have somewhere to get out of the wind. They still do."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "THE BURREN, AN BHOIRINN. 'The rocky place' in Irish. A Cromwellian officer wrote: 'not a tree to hang a man, nor water to drown him, nor soil enough to bury him.' He'd missed the flowers entirely."
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "ARCTIC AND ALPINE FLOWERS. On the Burren, spring gentian, mountain avens and Mediterranean orchids all bloom in the same cracks. Seeds were carried here after the Ice Age and nobody had the heart to leave."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "CLINTS AND GRYKES. The blocks of limestone are clints and the cracks between them are grykes. The Burren has a lot of both. Mind your ankles."
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 39,
   "plaque": "burren_turloughs",
   "title": "Turloughs",
   "text": "A turlough, from the Irish turlach, roughly 'dry lake', is a lake that comes and goes. In winter water wells up through the limestone and floods the hollow. In summer it drains away underground and cattle graze where the fish were."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "burren_matchmaking",
   "title": "Matchmaking",
   "text": "Every September the spa town of Lisdoonvarna, on the edge of the Burren, holds a matchmaking festival. Farmers once came after the harvest to find a wife, with help from the local matchmaker. These days it's mostly dancing and hopeful looks."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "landsky_karst",
   "title": "Karst",
   "text": "Country where the rock dissolves is called karst, after the Kras region of Slovenia. The Burren, from the Irish Boireann, a rocky place, is Ireland's great example: rain picks up carbon dioxide from the air and the soil, turns weakly acid, and eats the limestone, so streams vanish underground, caves open up, and lakes come and go with the seasons. The rock is the floor of a sea from about 330 million years ago, when Ireland lay near the equator."
  }
 ],
